WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.050
In this video, you create the largest virtual machine a single ARM64 host in SUSE Virtualization accepts: 512 virtual CPUs and 160 gigabytes of memory.

00:00:12.050 --> 00:00:20.310
Each host in this cluster is an Ampere Altra server with one hundred sixty cores and about one hundred twenty gigabytes of allocatable memory.

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:22.570
Go to Virtual Machines

00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:32.760
and select Create.

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:35.500
Choose the namespace

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:37.150
and a name.

00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:44.940
Enter five hundred twelve CPUs. That is the hard ceiling of the ARM64 virt machine type, even on a host with fewer cores.

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:49.520
Enter one hundred sixty gigabytes of memory, more than the host can allocate.

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:56.580
Memory is admitted against node allocatable times the memory overcommit ratio, one hundred fifty percent by default.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:03.080
So the virtual machine requests about one hundred seven gigabytes, plus its overhead, which still fits on one host.

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:04.920
On the Volumes tab,

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:10.420
select the openSUSE Leap ARM64 cloud image.

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:12.460
On Advanced Options,

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:18.080
select a cloud configuration template that creates a demo user with a console password.

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:21.930
Select Create.

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:29.240
The virtual machine is running with five hundred twelve CPUs and one hundred sixty gigabytes, on a single host.

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:32.870
The guest has finished booting. Open its console.

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:38.560
Log in as the demo user

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:43.630
and the password from the template.

00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:48.330
Check the processor count and the memory.

00:01:48.330 --> 00:01:55.460
The guest reports five hundred twelve processors and about one hundred sixty gigabytes of memory.

00:01:55.460 --> 00:02:07.720
Part two runs on a single x86 host. You raise the overcommit ratios, then boot one virtual machine with one thousand twenty-four CPUs and another with eight terabytes of memory.

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:16.460
This host is a Dell PowerEdge R640 with ninety-six CPU threads and about six hundred twenty-six gigabytes of allocatable memory.

00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:22.880
In Advanced, Settings, open the action menu of overcommit-config, which still holds the defaults,

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:27.130
and select Edit Setting to change how much the scheduler may promise.

00:02:27.130 --> 00:02:35.640
The scheduler reserves the CPU count divided by the ratio. Raise CPU to two thousand percent, so a thousand-CPU VM reserves about fifty-one cores.

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:41.590
Raise memory to fifteen hundred percent, so eight terabytes reserve about five hundred forty-six gigabytes.

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:45.570
Select Save to apply the new ratios.

00:02:45.570 --> 00:02:50.770
Now go to Virtual Machines and select Create for the first virtual machine.

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:55.790
Choose the demo namespace

00:02:55.790 --> 00:02:59.250
and name it demo-largest-cpu.

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:07.600
Enter one thousand twenty-four CPUs. Unlike ARM64, the x86 machine type has no five hundred twelve CPU wall.

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:13.150
Give it sixteen gigabytes of memory, and keep the default CPU model and BIOS firmware.

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:22.450
On the Volumes tab, select the SLES sixteen minimal cloud image on LVM thin storage.

00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:31.250
On Advanced Options, select a cloud configuration template that creates a demo user with a console password.

00:03:31.250 --> 00:03:35.510
Select Create to submit the first virtual machine.

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:43.440
Select Create again for the second virtual machine. In this lab, UEFI firmware did not start with a thousand CPUs.

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:50.010
In the demo namespace, name it demo-largest-mem.

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:53.070
Sixteen CPUs are enough for this one.

00:03:53.070 --> 00:03:58.340
Enter eight thousand one hundred ninety-two gigabytes, which is eight terabytes of memory.

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:06.140
Above about one terabyte, the default CPU model's address width is too narrow, so choose host-passthrough.

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:12.850
Also select Booting in EFI mode. Legacy BIOS can hang while it builds a multi-terabyte memory map.

00:04:14.510 --> 00:04:19.080
Select the same SLES image on the Volumes tab,

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:24.880
and the same login template on Advanced Options.

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:31.470
Select Create to submit the second virtual machine.

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:38.020
Both virtual machines are running on the one host. The guests need a few minutes to initialize.

00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:42.440
The guest of demo-largest-cpu has finished booting. Open its console.

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:48.100
Log in as the demo user

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:53.280
with the password from the template.

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:57.990
Check the processor count and the memory.

00:04:57.990 --> 00:05:03.520
The guest reports one thousand twenty-four processors on a host with ninety-six threads.

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:08.590
The guest of demo-largest-mem has finished booting. Open its console.

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:14.210
Log in as the demo user

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:19.390
with the password from the template.

00:05:19.390 --> 00:05:23.820
Run the same check in this guest.

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:31.030
This guest reports about eight terabytes of memory, on a host with seven hundred fifty-four gigabytes of RAM.

00:05:31.030 --> 00:05:44.328
Only a fraction of that memory can ever be backed by real RAM, and those CPUs share ninety-six threads, so treat these values as limits to know, not a sizing target. Next, you pack many small virtual machines onto one host.
